EXT. CREVASSE LEDGE - GLACIER - DAY

Blue-white walls. A thin shelf of ice a hundred feet below the surface. Wind howls overhead like a train that never arrives.

MARTA VOLK (30s, orange jacket torn at the shoulder, goggles cracked) lies on the ledge, one leg twisted under her. Her pack is split open beside her. A handheld voice recorder dangles from a strap on her wrist, its red light on.

She tries to sit. Gasps. Falls back.

MARTA
Okay. Okay okay okay.

She breathes into her glove. Steam curls. She reaches for the pack, grabs a thermos, shakes it. Empty. Tosses it down. It spins into the dark, clinks, falls silent for a very long time.

She stares after it.

MARTA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Don't look down.

She fumbles at the recorder, presses a button. A click, then her own voice, bright and young, plays back from earlier in the day.

MARTA (V.O.)
(recording)
Day four, summit approach. Weather's perfect. I'm going to make it to the col by noon and be back at base before the storm. Marta Volk, signing off.

The recording clicks off. Marta laughs, a ragged crack of a laugh.

MARTA
You liar.

She turns the recorder over. Presses record.

MARTA (CONT'D)
(into recorder)
Day four. Fell about ninety feet into a crevasse. Left leg broken. Maybe two ribs. Phone's dead. Rope's gone. It's three o'clock. I'm going to try to climb.

She stops the recording, studies the ice wall. Finds a handhold. Pulls herself up an inch, two, three inches. Screams. Slips back down. Lies there panting.

Silence. Wind. A crack echoes through the ice.

MARTA (CONT'D)
No no no.

A line of fracture races across the wall above her. Ice dust sifts. She rolls, covers her head. A slab of ice the size of a door crashes onto the ledge just beyond her feet and shatters, flinging shards across her jacket.

She lies still. Waits. Nothing else falls.

MARTA (CONT'D)
(a breath)
Okay.

Her hand gropes at her jacket and finds a small black pouch zipped into the lining. She pulls it out. Inside, a whistle and an emergency flare. She stares at them, laughing and crying.

MARTA (CONT'D)
You absolute idiot. It was in your pocket the whole time.

She jams the whistle in her teeth and blows. A long, shrill note spears up through the crevasse. She pulls the cap off the flare and strikes it. It hisses. Red light floods the blue walls, turns the ice to fire.

She raises the flare overhead and blows the whistle again, again.

Far above, faint, very faint, the drone of a helicopter rotor answers.

Marta drops her head to the ice. The flare sputters in her fist. She keeps blowing.
